Wc command (word count)

wc command in linux is used to count lines,words chars/bytesfrom a file

Syntax: wc <file-name>

Above command will display total no of lines,words and chars from the file

....................................

wc -l <file-name>

This command will display total no of line in the file

wc -w <file-name>

This command will display total no of words in the file

wc -m <file-name>

This command will display total no of chars/byts in the file

wc -L <file-name>

This command will display total no of chars in the longest line of the file

....................................................

Head command

Head command is used to display the first few lines of a file on the terminal

Note: By default head command will display the first 10 lines of a file on the terminal

Syntax: head <file-name>

Ex:head -12 <file-name>

Above command will display first 12 lines of a file on the terminal

Ex:head -6 <file-name>

Above command will display first 6 lines of a file on the terminal

..................................

Tail command

tail command is used to display the last few lines of a file on the terminal

Note: By default tail command will display the last 10 lines of a file on the terminal

Syntax: tail <file-name>

Ex:tail -12 <file-name>

Above command will display last 12 lines of a file on the terminal

Ex:tail -6 <file-name>

Above command will display last 6 lines of a file on the terminal

...............................................

Pipe operator (| )

The pipe operator is used to send the output of one command as an input to another command

Syntax: command-1 | command-2

........................................

Ex: head -15 <file-name> | tail -4

Above command display line no 12 to 15 on the terminal

Ex: head <file-name> | tail -1

Above command display only 10th line on the terminal

Ex: head -12 <file-name> | tail -8

Above command display line no 5 to 12 on the terminal

Ex: tail -15 <file-name> | tail -1

Above command display only the 15th line  on the terminal

Ex: tail -4 <file-name> | head -1

Above command display only first line out of last 4 lines of a file

Ex: cat -n <file-name> | tail -3

Above command will display last 3 lines of a file along with line numbers

............................

tee command

tee command is used with pipe operator which is used to read the input and copy to one or more files

Syntax: command-1 | tee command-2

Ex: cat class3.txt | tee fil5.txt

Above command will replace the content of file5.txt with the content of class3.txt

Ex: ls | tee file.txt
Above command will move the output of ls to file.txt

Ex: tail -8 file4.txt | tee rk.txt

Above command will copy last 8 lines of file4.txt into rk.txt

